{"title":"Phi","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p><b>Phi</b> (in lowercase, either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/1/2c175f60eecef1de7560c3bdea495d69f26f719d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/1/921aade3100afbc11d0d13f71cdd7e68d83ad52e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\varphi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"11\" height=\"11\" />; capitalized, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/5/b85bbfb165b853d6f5f9734be4cdabbb453f4e43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Phi$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" />) is the 21st letter in the Greek alphabet.  It is used frequently in mathematical writing, often to represent the constant <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/c/93c2b2e9d216af3f6a5b1536d99a69aa9844a02a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1+\\sqrt{5}}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"58\" height=\"41\" />. (The Greek letter <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Tau\" title=\"Tau\">Tau</a> (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/c/1dc1c0119a604b91be9142370dc3159b6a9bbcb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tau$\" width=\"9\" height=\"8\" />) was also used for this purpose in pre-Renaissance times.)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Uses\">Uses</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Phi&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Uses\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/1/2c175f60eecef1de7560c3bdea495d69f26f719d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is also commonly used to represent <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler%27s_totient_function\" title=\"Euler&#39;s totient function\">Euler's totient function</a>.</li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/1/2c175f60eecef1de7560c3bdea495d69f26f719d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is used to express the golden ratio.</li></ul>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/1/2c175f60eecef1de7560c3bdea495d69f26f719d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> appears in a variety of different mathematical contexts:\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Golden_ratio\">Golden ratio</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Phi&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Golden ratio\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/1/2c175f60eecef1de7560c3bdea495d69f26f719d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is also known as the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Golden_ratio\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Golden ratio\">golden ratio</a>. It was commonly believed by the Greeks to be the most aesthetically pleasing ratio between side lengths in a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rectangle\" title=\"Rectangle\">rectangle</a>.  The <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Golden_rectangle\" title=\"Golden rectangle\">golden rectangle</a> is a rectangle with side lengths of 1 and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/1/2c175f60eecef1de7560c3bdea495d69f26f719d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" />; it has a number of interesting properties. The golden ratio is also equal to the positive solution of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Quadratic_equation\" title=\"Quadratic equation\">quadratic equation</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/c/32c76b2637ed1588f05720785daa5cdf9c09e2d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2-x-1=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"114\" height=\"15\" /> as well as the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Continued_fraction&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Continued fraction (page does not exist)\">continued fraction</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/4/614fab24c2a147ed9ef6136f1e6d4ba4036ba61b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+\\frac{1}{1+\\frac{1}{1+\\frac{1}{1+\\frac{1}{1+\\cdots}}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -31px\" width=\"133\" height=\"56\" /> and the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nested_radical&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Nested radical (page does not exist)\">continued radical</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/5/065455868b5328114df32b070a2a8f5ec53ee814.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{1+\\sqrt{1+\\sqrt{1+\\sqrt{1+\\cdots}}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"215\" height=\"53\" />. It is the only positive real number that is one more than its <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Perfect_square\" title=\"Perfect square\">square</a> and one more than its <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Reciprocal\" title=\"Reciprocal\">reciprocal</a>. It is also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/4/7148265a4b728b66f62dc5284681031e3e160a16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${\\lim_{x \\to \\infty}} \\frac{F_{x+1}}{F_x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"73\" height=\"40\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/4/b842b4552478b69732a9aea702309d8c51e43056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> is the nth number in the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Fibonacci_sequence\" title=\"Fibonacci sequence\">Fibonacci sequence</a>. In other words, if you divide the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/5/535793effa45c63b7b14570988b23dea07c508cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n^th$\" width=\"27\" height=\"15\" /> term of the Fibonacci series over the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/9/169a7316d5a7cbd757b078256237c08c675f66ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n-1)^th$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"75\" height=\"21\" /> term, the result approaches <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/1/2c175f60eecef1de7560c3bdea495d69f26f719d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> increases. In fact, if you make a fibonacci-like equation starting with any two numbers, as long as they aren't both zero, it will converge to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/1/921aade3100afbc11d0d13f71cdd7e68d83ad52e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\varphi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"11\" height=\"11\" />. The first fifteen digits of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/1/2c175f60eecef1de7560c3bdea495d69f26f719d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> in decimal representation are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/f/90f86709e7cd24e86a85934935835d64edbb59fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1.61803398874989$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"140\" height=\"13\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Phi&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Irrational_number\" title=\"Irrational number\">Irrational number</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Geometry\" title=\"Geometry\">Geometry</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Zeckendorf_representation\" title=\"Zeckendorf representation\">Zeckendorf representation</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Phi&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018085729\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.025 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.045 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 136/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 250/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 3941/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.666      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.666      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:1891:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018085729 and revision id 274371. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}